<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Molokans</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Molokans are a Russian Spiritual Christian sect that emerged in the 18th century and rejects the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox Church</a>'s priesthood, icons and sacraments. Persecuted in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>, many were resettled in the 19th century in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-caucasus">Transcaucasia</a>, including the Mughan steppe and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>. Some emigrated to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in the early 20th century, settling especially in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/molokans.jpg" alt="Molokans" width="320" height="265" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kennan, George, 1845-1924 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Molokane_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Molokans are a Russian Spiritual Christian sect that evolved from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodoxy">Eastern Orthodoxy</a> in the East Slavic lands. Their name, from the Russian moloko, milk, is an exonym used by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> neighbours, who regarded them as heretics because they drank milk during the Orthodox fasts. They tend to call themselves Spiritual Christians. The term Molokan is first recorded in the 1670s, but it referred to a group not directly related to the later Molokans.</p><p>The Molokans of today split from the Doukhobors in 1779 and 1780, thinking that the Doukhobors neglected the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a>. Their founder, Semyon Matveevich Uklein, was the son-in-law of the Doukhobor leader Ilarion Poberokhin, and he insisted on reliance on the Gospel texts. Uklein's Molokans from Tambov spread the faith in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orenburg">Orenburg</a>, Saratov and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astrakhan">Astrakhan</a> provinces, and he went on organising congregations until his death in 1809.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>From the intervention of Count Nikolay Zubov in 1795 the Molokans were tolerated under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine the Great</a>, but within strict rules meant to curb their growth; those who ignored them were punished as heretics. Forbidden to win converts, they turned to endogamy. The government sent them away from central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> in 1833, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> in 1834, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> in the 1830s, and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a>. In 1833 a schism produced the Jumpers, who reported charismatic outpourings of the Holy Spirit, while the old Molokans were called Constants. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of 1912 counted 133,935 Molokane and 4,844 Pryguny in Russia.</p><p>Roughly 3,500 Molokans left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> between 1901 and 1911, many settling at Potrero Hill in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-francisco">San Francisco</a> and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/los-angeles">Los Angeles</a>. In August 1917 thirty-five men who refused to register under the Selective Service Act of 1917 were sentenced to a year in prison. Jumper communities also settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>'s Guadalupe Valley.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Molokans hold the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> as their main guide and read it spiritually, treating sacraments such as water baptism as signs of spiritual truths. They reject the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> priesthood, icons and the cross, elect councils of elders, advocate pacifism, hold meetings in homes and do not drink or smoke. They follow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-testament">Old Testament</a> laws, avoiding pork and shellfish, and do not obey Orthodox fasting rules. Beliefs varied sharply among their groups; some built chapels or revered saints and icons.</p><p>About 20,000 people in the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> identify as Molokans, at least ethnically, with some 200 churches. About 25,000 live in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, mostly around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/los-angeles">Los Angeles</a> and Fresno. There are also communities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruguay">Uruguay</a>. In the United States they keep Russian in church services and hold community dinners called obed.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Molokans” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The republic was based on councils of Russian settler villages, including <b>Molokans</b>, and sought to join Soviet Russia.</blockquote><a href="/state/mughan-soviet-republic">Mughan Soviet Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>The Mughan steppe and Lankaran area had many Russian settler villages, including <b>Molokans</b> and Old Believers, established in the 19th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/provisional-military-dictatorship-of-mughan">Provisional Military Dictatorship of Mughan</a></li></ul></section>
